---
/**
 * Four moments, one per link in the chain the hero band draws. It used to be
 * three and all three sat in the front half (price, order math, margin), which
 * made the page read as a quoting tool. The manual work being replaced is not
 * quoting; it is the whole loop, and the buying half is where most of the
 * retyping actually happens. The fourth moment is the purchase order and the
 * short delivery, so the problem statement covers the same ground the product
 * does.
 *
 * Order matters: it walks PRICE IN -> ORDER -> BUY -> THE MONTH, the same
 * direction as the hero band and src/lib/workflow.ts.
 *
 * THE SHEET IS NAMED IN THE TICKETS SINCE 2026-09-05, at the owner's request to
 * make the spreadsheet pain explicit. The heading already carried it; the four
 * moments, which is where the eye lands, did not, so the diagnosis read as
 * generic kitchen admin. Each body now names the artifact: the copy, the four
 * tabs, the rows retyped, the folder. RC-56.
 *
 * WHAT NO LINE HERE MAY DO IS DELIVER A VERDICT ON THE TOOL. Denying a
 * spreadsheet the ability to re-cost a recipe would be simply untrue: a lookup
 * against a price tab does exactly that. What is true is that the reader's COPY
 * holds the old number and that she retypes it. Every sentence therefore
 * describes her own hands, never the software's limits. RC-40 and RC-47 add the
 * second boundary: no product is named outside /compare, so the noun stays
 * generic. check-landing-claims.mjs enforces both. The phrasing of this note is
 * deliberate: the scan reads comments as well as copy, so spelling the banned
 * sentence out here, even as an example, would fail the build.
 */
import SectionHandoff from '../SectionHandoff.astro';

const moments = [
	{
		title: 'You quote from an old price',
		body: 'The vendor moved the case price and you changed it where you saw it. The sheet you quoted this job from is a copy of last month’s, and it still holds the old one.'
	},
	{
		title: 'You rebuild the same order four times',
		body: 'Forty more guests, and the same number has to change in the recipe math, the shopping list, the prep plan and the pack check. Four edits, and the one you miss turns up at the dock.'
	},
	{
		title: 'You retype the list to buy it',
		body: 'You copy the rows out by hand: one message per supplier, then a checklist at the dock, then whatever the truck was short, typed back in.'
	},
	{
		title: 'You learn the margin after service',
		body: 'The invoices arrive after the event and go in a folder, not in the sheet you quoted from. Weeks later, if you go and check, they decide whether the price worked.'
	}
];
---

<!-- story: docs/stories/homepage-visual-guidance.story.md -->
<!-- story: docs/stories/homepage-spreadsheet-pain.story.md -->
<!-- TWO COLUMNS SINCE 2026-08-29. This section was a text column on the left
     and forty percent of the viewport empty on the right at 1440, then four
     more text columns under it. The four moments now sit in the right column
     as a stack of tickets (the brand's one accent device, tilted the brand's
     1.5 degrees), so the eye has somewhere to land after the heading and the
     section is one frame rather than two. No image: the claim here is the
     reader's week, and no screenshot of ours can show that. -->
<section id="problem" class="border-y border-hairline bg-cream">
	<div class="container-page py-section-tight">
		<div class="grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-[6fr_5fr] lg:gap-16">
		<div class="max-w-3xl" data-reveal>
			<!-- Eyebrow added 2026-08-26 as part of the page-wide role register.
			     See the note in src/pages/index.astro for the measurement. -->
			<p class="eyebrow">What goes wrong</p>
			<h2 class="mt-4 text-h2 font-semibold">The spreadsheet works until the job changes.</h2>
			<p class="mt-5 max-w-[68ch] text-lede text-ink-soft">
				Most kitchens cost on a spreadsheet, and it holds until something moves. A supplier
				price changes. The client adds forty guests. A case arrives short. Each one is a
				number somebody has to find and change by hand, in every copy of the sheet that
				still holds the old one.
			</p>
			<!-- THE FLAW, added 2026-08-27. Everything above this line diagnoses the
			     TOOL; this line describes the reader, which is the difference between a
			     page that explains a problem and a page someone recognises themselves
			     in. Written without blame on purpose: starting from last job's sheet is
			     the correct move with the tools most kitchens have, and a reader told
			     otherwise stops reading. STORY-SPINE-2026-08-27.md, worksheet 2. -->
			<p class="mt-5 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-soft">
				So every job starts as the last job&rsquo;s sheet, saved under a new name. That is
				the right move with the tools most kitchens have. It is also how the price you
				quote and the price you pay drift apart without anybody getting anything wrong.
			</p>
			<!-- The provenance claim, surfaced here as well as in BuiltForKitchens. It
			     is the strongest credibility asset the page owns and it used to appear
			     only at 78% depth, which meant the reader evaluated the whole diagnosis
			     before learning who made it. One line, not a relocation: the full block
			     with the photograph stays where it is. -->
			<p class="mt-5 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-soft">
				I cooked professionally for twelve years before I wrote any of this. Those four
				are my own week, not a survey. The last one is the one I built this for.
			</p>
		</div>

		<!-- The four moments as a ticket stack. Each is a real <li>; the tilt and
		     the overlap are decoration only, cleared below lg where they would
		     eat the column. Reveal stagger runs top to bottom so the stack lands
		     like paper dealt onto a counter. -->
		<ol role="list" class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-1 lg:gap-0">
			{
				moments.map((moment, index) => (
					<li
						class:list={[
							'border border-ticket-rule bg-paper px-5 py-4 shadow-ticket',
							index > 0 && 'lg:-mt-2',
							index % 2 === 0 ? 'lg:rotate-[-1.5deg]' : 'lg:rotate-[1.5deg]',
							index === 1 && 'lg:ml-6',
							index === 3 && 'lg:ml-4'
						]}
						data-reveal
						style={`--reveal-delay: ${index * 90}ms`}
					>
						<p class="font-mono text-eyebrow font-semibold tracking-wide text-amber-deep">
							{String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')}
						</p>
						<h3 class="mt-1 text-h3 font-semibold">{moment.title}</h3>
						<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">{moment.body}</p>
					</li>
				))
			}
		</ol>
		</div>

		<div class="wrong-not-slow" data-reveal>
			<p class="eyebrow">And the quiet one</p>
			<p class="mt-4 max-w-[72ch] text-lede text-ink-soft">
				Those four are the slow ones. The one that costs money is quieter than any of them.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-5 max-w-[72ch] text-ink-soft">
				A sheet totals what is in it. Leave the beef blank while you wait on the invoice
				and the plate cost still prints, short by the price of the beef, looking exactly
				as confident as a right answer. A conversion does the same thing: the recipe is in
				grams, the pack is in pounds, and the number bridging them was typed once by
				somebody who is not in the kitchen today.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-5 max-w-[72ch] text-ink-soft">
				Those two are not slower. They are wrong, and nothing on the screen says so.
				CostCook stops instead. A missing price or a missing conversion holds the costing
				and names the line it is waiting on, rather than totalling around it and handing
				you a number.
			</p>
		</div>

		<SectionHandoff from="problem" />
	</div>
</section>

<style>
	/* Rule-and-prose register, deliberately unlike the ticket stack above it:
	   full width under both columns, one hairline, no number, no tilt, no card.
	   The reader should feel the subject change rather than read a fifth item. */
	.wrong-not-slow {
		margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem);
		padding-top: 2rem;
		border-top: 1px solid var(--color-ticket-rule);
	}
</style>

